.TH ROOT 3
.SH NAME
root \- the root file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #/
.nf

.B /bin
.B /dev
.B /env
.B /lib
.B /mnt
.B /n
.B /net
.B /proc
.B /srv
.B /usr
.B /ws
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I root
device serves the one-level tree that becomes
.B /
in the name space the kernel builds at boot.
Almost every name in it is an empty directory, a place to
.IR bind (2)
onto rather than a file with contents of its own.
.PP
.B #/
is an ordinary device, registered in the device table like any other (see
.IR intro (3)),
and reachable by that name.
This is unlike Plan 9, where
.B #/
is refused outright: here it is how the kernel itself rebuilds a name
space from nothing, after a fork left one with no root to walk from.
A process is not expected to attach
.B #/
directly.
The boot name space already has it mounted at
.BR / .
.PP
By convention rather than enforcement, the kernel binds a device or a
served tree onto each of these names before starting anything: the console
and disks at
.BR /dev ,
posted services at
.BR /srv ,
environment variables at
.BR /env ,
processes at
.BR /proc ,
the boot disk's programs and files under
.B /bin
and
.BR /lib ,
and the disk the machine started from at
.BR /n/esp .
Nothing stops a process from binding something else over any of them.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/vfs/root.odin
